The '''Monument to the Ones'''<ref name="CGttSWG">''[[Star Wars Encyclopedia: The Comprehensive Guide to the Star Wars Galaxy]]''</ref> was an ancient<ref name="Season 2 announcement">{{SW|url=news/ahsoka-season-2-dave-filoni-sketch|text=''Ahsoka'' Season 2 in Development, New Sketch Revealed}}</ref> monument depicting [[the Ones]], also known as the [[Mortis]] gods, located on the [[extragalactic]] [[planet]] of [[Peridea]]. The sculpture was carved into the face of a rocky mountain, overlooking a valley. The [[Father (Mortis)|Father]] was depicted with his arm outstretched, pointing toward a snowy mountain range on the opposite side of the valley. The sculpture of the [[Son]] stood on the Father's left.<ref name="Part 8" /> A sculpture of the [[Daughter]]<ref name="Celebration">{{SWYouTube|q0trVP56Cy0|Star Wars Celebration LIVE! 2025 - DAY 2}}</ref> stood to the Father's right, but was missing its head.<ref name="Part 8" /> In [[9 ABY]],<ref name="Ahsoka Date">{{AhsokaSeriesDate}}</ref> former [[Jedi Knight]] [[Baylan Skoll]] traveled to the sculpture, in search of something he sensed "stirring" on the planet.<ref name="Part 8" />

{{Struct-stub}}

==Behind the scenes==
The Monument to the Ones first appeared in [[Part Eight: The Jedi, the Witch, and the Warlord|the eight and last episode]] of the [[Ahsoka Season 1|first season]] of the live-action television series ''[[Star Wars: Ahsoka]]'', directed by series' creator [[Dave Filoni]]<ref name="Part 8" /> and released on [[October 3]], [[2023]].<ref name="Date">{{Twitter|starwars|status/1692552080764887366|Star Wars|date=2023-08-18|quote=.Thank you to all the fans who celebrated with us at our @AhsokaOfficial fan events around the world last night! We're excited to announce that new episodes of #Ahsoka will now launch Tuesdays at 6PM PT, starting with our two-episode premiere on August 22, only on @DisneyPlus.|archivedate=20230818152836}}</ref> It was then later first identified in the [[2024]] reference book ''[[Star Wars Encyclopedia: The Comprehensive Guide to the Star Wars Galaxy]]''.<ref name="CGttSWG" /> A sketch drawn by Filoni that was revealed at the official announcement of [[Ahsoka Season 2|''Ahsoka Season'' 2]] showed [[Ahsoka Tano]] and [[Sabine Wren]] standing on top of the outstretched index finger of the Father overlooking Peredia.<ref name="Sketch">{{SW|url=news/ahsoka-season-2-dave-filoni-sketch|text=''Ahsoka'' Season 2 in Development, New Sketch Revealed}}</ref>

The statue of the Father with it's outstretched hand is reminiscent of the statues, named the Gates of Argonath or Pillars of the Kings, that are standing at the shores of the river [[Wikipedia:Anduin|Anduin]] at the entrance of the lands of [[Wikipedia:Gondor|Gondor]] in the ''[[Wikipedia:The Lord of the Rings|The Lord of the Rings]]'' novels by [[Wikipedia:J. R. R. Tolkien|J. R. R. Tolkien]]<ref name="LotR">''[[Wikipedia:The Lord of the Rings|The Lord of the Rings]]''</ref> and it's [[Wikipedia:The Lord of the Rings (film series)|film adaptions]],<ref name="LotR RotK film">[[Wikipedia:The Lord of the Rings: The Return of the King (film)|''The Lord of the Rings: The Return of the King'' (2003 film)]]</ref> of which Filoni is a fan of and which have served as inspiration for Ahsoka Tano in the series.<ref name="Best of Jedi">{{SW|url=news/rosario-dawson-ahsoka-tano-interview|text=The Best of the Jedi: Rosario Dawson on Bringing Ahsoka Tano to Live-Action and ''The Mandalorian''}}</ref> Similarly, other parts of Peredia like the [[Great Mothers' Fortress]] and the general terrain of the grasslands are also reminiscent<ref name="Part 8" /> of the depictions of Gondor.<ref name="LotR RotK film" />

